---

Every [release](https://github.com/highercomve/nomadfs/releases/latest) has
the app for Android (an APK) and Linux (AppImage, deb and rpm), and the
`nomadfs` command line for Linux. On Android, allow installing from your
browser or file manager when it asks.

On Linux, this installs the latest release for your user (the app's AppImage
and the command line in `~/.local/bin`, and a launcher entry), checking every
file against the release's checksums:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/highercomve/nomadfs/main/scripts/install.sh | bash
```

`VERSION=0.3.5` installs a given release; `NO_CLI=1`, `NO_DESKTOP=1` and
`NO_RESTART=1` skip the command line, the launcher entry, or starting the app.
The app then updates itself ([Updates]($link.ref('updates'))).

To build from source you need [Zig 0.16.0](https://ziglang.org/download/).
For the Linux mount you also need `fusermount3` (the `fuse3` package on most
distributions).

# [Build the node]($heading.id('build'))

From the repository root:

```sh
zig build          # zig-out/bin/nomadfs: the node and the command line
zig build test     # module, executable and integration tests
```

`zig build lib` installs only the C ABI library the mobile bindings use.

# [Write a configuration]($heading.id('config'))

Every command reads `nomadfs.conf`, looking in this order: the current folder,
`~/.nomadfs/nomadfs.conf`, `/etc/nomadfs/nomadfs.conf`, then
`/usr/share/nomadfs/nomadfs.conf`. A path given on the command line (or with
`--config`) wins over all of them.

```ini
[node]
nickname = "Laptop"
# 64 hex characters: `openssl rand -hex 32` (or `make gen-swarm-key`)
swarm_key = "…"

[storage]
storage_path = "/home/me/.nomadfs/data"

[network]
port = 9000
bootstrap_peers = []

# Folders this device shares, repeatable
share = "Documents:/home/me/Documents"
```

`nickname`, `swarm_key` and `storage_path` are required. The swarm key is what
makes devices one swarm: every device that holds it can reach the others. Keep
it private; to add devices you will normally use an
[invite]($link.page('docs/connecting-devices')), which hands the key over
without showing it.

The node's keys are created on first run: `node.key` (transport) and
`device.key` (the device's Ed25519 identity), with `trust.store` next to them.
They live in the current folder when a `device.key` is already there, otherwise
in `~/.nomadfs`. Set `key_path` to the node key's file to keep all three in one
known place.

# [Run it and share a folder]($heading.id('run'))

```sh
./zig-out/bin/nomadfs --share Photos:/home/me/Pictures
```

`--share Name:/path` adds to the shares in the configuration and can be
repeated. The node starts listening on the configured port and indexes each
share in the background:

```
NomadFS Node: Laptop (Storage: true)
Listening on port: 9000
Sharing 'Photos' from /home/me/Pictures (indexing in the background)
```

The node asks the router for a port mapping with UPnP when it can; set
`upnp_enabled = false` to turn that off.

# [Add a second device]($heading.id('second-device'))

With the node running, open a one-time invite in another terminal:

```sh
./zig-out/bin/nomadfs --invite
```

It prints a QR code and a code like
`nomadfs://invite?addr=192.168.1.20:9001&token=…`, then waits. On the other
device, open the NomadFS app, go to **Connect devices** and either paste the
code under **Join with an invite** or tap **Scan QR code** (on Android). The
invite works once and expires after 10 minutes. Both devices end up trusting
each other, and the new one receives the swarm key and the address to reconnect
to. [Connecting devices]($link.page('docs/connecting-devices')) explains what
happens on the wire.

Two machines that only run the command line can join the same swarm by sharing
the same `swarm_key` and listing each other in `bootstrap_peers`:

```ini
bootstrap_peers = ["tcp://192.168.1.20:9000"]
```

# [Mount everything (Linux)]($heading.id('mount'))

```sh
mkdir -p ~/NomadFS
./zig-out/bin/nomadfs --mount ~/NomadFS
```

This runs the node and mounts every reachable device's shares at
`~/NomadFS/<device>/<share>` and the swarm folder at `~/NomadFS/Swarm`. Ctrl-C
unmounts. See [Shared folders & mount]($link.page('docs/shared-folders')).

# [Build the app]($heading.id('app'))

The app in `nomadfs-gui/` is built with [Oriel](https://oriel.foreachdeveloper.com)
**v0.20.0**, which `zig build` fetches:

```sh
cd nomadfs-gui
zig build run        # build and run the desktop app
zig build package    # deb, rpm and AppImage in zig-out/package/
oriel android build  # an APK for arm64-v8a and x86_64
```

With the `oriel` CLI installed, `oriel run`, `oriel build` and `oriel package`
do the same as the `zig build` steps. On Android the app asks for access to all
files so it can read the folders you share.

# [Updates]($heading.id('updates'))

The app checks the GitHub releases shortly after it starts and every few hours
(**About** has the switch, and a **Check for updates** button). Every release
is signed, and the app only accepts updates signed with the NomadFS release key.

- **AppImage**: **Update** downloads the new version, checks it, replaces the
  AppImage and restarts the app.
- **Android**: **Download** opens the new APK in the browser; open it to
  install it over the current one.
- **deb or rpm**: update with your package manager, or download the new package
  from the release page the app links.

# [Keep the phone reachable (Android)]($heading.id('background'))

Android stops apps you aren't using, and a stopped phone drops out of the
swarm: your other devices can't browse its shares and the swarm folder waits
until you open the app. Turn on **Settings → Background → Keep running in the
background**: NomadFS keeps running with a "NomadFS is running" notification,
also after you swipe it away from Recents. Its **Stop** button turns it off.

Battery optimization can still stop it, Xiaomi phones sooner than most.
**Battery & autostart settings** opens NomadFS's page in the system settings:
set the battery to **No restrictions** (or Unrestricted), and on Xiaomi turn
on **Autostart**.
